---
import { PROVIDERS } from '../data'
---

<section id="byok" class="border-b border-[#1c211d] bg-[#0e1210]">
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-6 py-16">
    <div
      class="inline-flex items-center rounded-full border border-[#2c5842] bg-[#10241b] px-3 py-1 font-mono text-xs text-[#5fdba0]"
    >
      model-agnostic
    </div>
    <h2 class="mt-4 text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Bring whichever judge you trust.</h2>
    <p class="mt-4 max-w-2xl text-[#9aa39d]">
      leanest's selection judgment is pluggable. It never assumes one vendor, one hosted API, or
      one model: pick a provider with <code class="font-mono text-[#c9d1cb]">LEANEST_PROVIDER</code
      >, or leave it on the free default. Adding a new one is one adapter behind the same interface,
      not a rewrite.
    </p>

    <div class="mt-10 grid grid-cols-1 gap-6 md:grid-cols-3">
      {
        PROVIDERS.map((p) => (
          <div class="rounded-lg border border-[#232623] bg-[#121513] p-5">
            <div class="flex items-center gap-2">
              <code class="font-mono text-sm text-[#7cf0b6]">{p.name}</code>
              {p.default && (
                <span class="rounded-full border border-[#2c5842] bg-[#10241b] px-2 py-0.5 font-mono text-[10px] text-[#5fdba0]">
                  default
                </span>
              )}
            </div>
            <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-[#9aa39d]">{p.how}</p>
            <p class="mt-3 font-mono text-xs text-[#7c8a80]">key: {p.key}</p>
          </div>
        ))
      }
    </div>

    <div class="mt-12 grid grid-cols-1 gap-10 md:grid-cols-2">
      <div>
        <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold tracking-tight">Fails open, always.</h3>
        <p class="mt-3 text-[#9aa39d]">
          leanest has no server of its own. Whichever provider you pick, the CLI calls it directly
          with your credentials, if it needs any. Nothing routes through leanest's infrastructure,
          because there isn't any.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-4 text-[#9aa39d]">
          No key, an expired one, a timeout, or an unknown provider name all resolve the same way:
          leanest runs the full suite and prints why, instead of silently skipping coverage.
        </p>
        <pre
          class="mt-6 rounded-lg border border-[#232623] bg-[#121513] px-4 py-3 font-mono text-[13px] break-words whitespace-pre-wrap text-[#c9d1cb]">⚠ Judge unavailable (...), running the full suite.</pre>
      </div>

      <div>
        <div class="font-mono text-xs tracking-wide text-[#7c8a80]">using jev instead of the default</div>
        <pre
          class="mt-3 rounded-lg border border-[#232623] bg-[#121513] px-4 py-3 font-mono text-[13px] break-words whitespace-pre-wrap text-[#c9d1cb]">gh secret set TYPESAFE_API_KEY --repo you/your-repo</pre>
        <div class="mt-6 font-mono text-xs tracking-wide text-[#7c8a80]">
          reference it in the workflow
        </div>
        <pre
          class="mt-3 rounded-lg border border-[#232623] bg-[#121513] px-4 py-3 font-mono text-[13px] break-words whitespace-pre-wrap text-[#c9d1cb]">provider: jev
typesafe-api-key: {'${{ secrets.TYPESAFE_API_KEY }}'}</pre>
        <p class="mt-4 text-sm text-[#7c8a80]">
          The default classifier-dev provider needs no secret at all, which also means
          forked-repo pull requests (no access to your repo's secrets) still get selection.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
